(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-3-oral-cavity-salivary-vignettes"]={"title":"Oral Cavity and Salivary Glands Vignettes 1 — CMS I Exam 3","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 3","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 31-year-old man has recurrent painful oral ulcers. He also has similar ulcers on the scrotum and has had episodes of eye inflammation and joint pain.","choices":["Behcet syndrome","Recurrent aphthous stomatitis","Herpes simplex infection","Systemic lupus erythematosus"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Oral ulcers are the commonest feature of Behcet, affecting up to 100 per cent, and genital ulcers occur in about 75 per cent and look identical to oral aphthae. Diagnosis is clinical: recurrent aphthous ulceration in the context of characteristic systemic manifestations, which here include ocular and articular involvement. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 19"},{"q":"A 60-year-old man with untreated periodontal disease asks whether it affects anything beyond his mouth.","choices":["It affects only the teeth and gums","It causes hearing loss","It only matters during pregnancy","It raises the risk of endocarditis and other systemic disease"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Periodontal disease is linked to heart disease and stroke through endocarditis risk, to respiratory disease through pneumonia, to osteoporosis through decreased bone density, and in men to kidney, pancreatic and blood cancers. It is also, with caries, a primary cause of tooth loss. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 119"},{"q":"A 9-year-old has cervical adenitis with a fluctuant area that has not settled on antibiotics.","choices":["Incision and drainage","A longer antibiotic course","Excisional biopsy","Observation alone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Management is directed at the underlying cause, and where an abscess has formed drainage is required. Antibiotics penetrate an established collection poorly, so fluctuance that persists through treatment is the signal to drain rather than to escalate the drug. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 94"},{"q":"A 46-year-old man develops fever, sore throat and neck stiffness a week after swallowing a fishbone.","choices":["Acute epiglottitis","Retropharyngeal abscess","Peritonsillar abscess","Ludwig angina"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. In adults the abscess usually follows intraoral procedures, trauma, a foreign body such as a fishbone, immunocompromise or extension from an odontogenic infection, rather than the nodal suppuration seen in children under five. It remains a surgical emergency. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 103"},{"q":"A 50-year-old woman has recurrent submandibular stones and wants to avoid losing the gland.","choices":["Intraoral extraction","Conservative management indefinitely","Sialoendoscopy","Gland excision"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Sialoendoscopy is the most recent minimally invasive option and is specifically described as able to avoid removal of a gland. It can be combined with open sialolithotomy where needed, which makes it the right answer for a patient whose priority is preserving the gland. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 43"},{"q":"A clinician is describing a cervical node and notes it is immobile and non-tender.","choices":["Features suggesting inflammation","Features suggesting a cyst","Features suggesting a vascular lesion","Features suggesting malignancy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The descriptors that matter are size, shape, mobility, consistency and tenderness. Immobility suggests fixation to surrounding structures by tumour, and non-tenderness points away from inflammation, which hurts. Persistence or continued enlargement is what triggers fine needle aspiration. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 93"},{"q":"A 58-year-old smoker has been hoarse for seven weeks with no pain and no other symptoms.","choices":["Refer for laryngoscopy","Trial a proton pump inhibitor","Voice rest and reassurance","A course of antibiotics"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Voice disturbance beyond two weeks is chronic laryngitis, which is not a true diagnosis but a description obliging a search for the cause. Laryngeal cancer and vocal cord polyps must be excluded, particularly in a smoker, so the stated step is referral for laryngoscopy. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 58"},{"q":"A 6-year-old has a unilateral, solitary, tender anterior cervical node following a sore throat.","choices":["Mycobacterium tuberculosis","Beta-haemolytic streptococcus, in about 70 per cent","Staphylococcus aureus, in the majority","Bartonella henselae"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Local ear, nose and throat infections drain to regional nodes, and about 70 per cent of these unilateral solitary anterior nodes relate to beta-haemolytic streptococcal infection, with 20 per cent staphylococcal including MRSA and 10 per cent viral, atypical mycobacterial or Bartonella. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 92"},{"q":"A 47-year-old man with poorly controlled diabetes has two days of increasing swelling under the chin and in the floor of the mouth after a dental infection. His tongue is pushed upward and backward and he is drooling.","choices":["Oral antibiotics and dental review in the morning","Drain the tooth abscess alone","Intravenous corticosteroids alone","Secure the airway, then antibiotics and drainage if threatened"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Ludwig angina is a rapidly spreading cellulitis of the submental, sublingual and submandibular spaces. Swelling of the floor of the mouth pushes the tongue up and back into the airway, which is why airway management comes first, with penicillin and metronidazole or ampicillin-sulbactam and external drainage through bilateral submental incisions if needed. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 110"},{"q":"An 81-year-old woman four days after hip surgery, with poor oral intake, develops painful swelling over the angle of the jaw with overlying erythema. Pus can be expressed from the duct opposite the upper second molar.","choices":["Antiviral therapy for mumps","Immediate parotidectomy","Sialography to look for a stone","Rehydration with antistaphylococcal antibiotics"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Dehydration causes salivary stasis, which lets bacteria ascend the parotid duct. Staphylococcus aureus is the commonest organism, and pus expressible from Stensen duct confirms the gland is infected. Treatment is rehydration to restore flow plus penicillinase-resistant gram-positive cover, with warm compresses, massage and sialogogues. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 38"},{"q":"A 44-year-old man has a diffuse greyish-white change of both buccal mucosae. Stretching the mucosa makes it disappear.","choices":["Oral lichen planus","Leukoedema","Leukoplakia","Oral candidiasis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Leukoedema is a normal variant caused by fluid accumulating within epithelial cells, and stretching disperses it so the change vanishes. That single manoeuvre separates a reassurance from the biopsy that leukoplakia would need. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 9"},{"q":"A 16-year-old treated with penicillin for pharyngitis four days ago returns unable to drink, with excruciating pain on swallowing and a muffled voice. The soft palate is deviated medially on the right with marked tonsillar swelling.","choices":["Infectious mononucleosis","Retropharyngeal abscess","Ludwig angina","Peritonsillar abscess"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Pus between the tonsillar capsule and the pharyngeal muscles displaces the soft palate and tonsil medially, and the muffled hot potato voice comes from the mass distorting the oropharynx. It is the commonest deep head and neck infection and typically follows inadequately treated tonsillitis. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 98"},{"q":"A 55-year-old man who chews betel nut and smokes asks about his risk of oral cancer.","choices":["Only alcohol matters","Both are recognised risk factors","Only smoking matters; betel nut is harmless","Neither is a risk factor for oral cancer"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The risk factors are tobacco chewed and smoked, alcohol, betel nut chewing, poor oral hygiene and immunosuppression. Squamous cell carcinoma is the commonest malignancy of the oral cavity and oropharynx, and prevention rests on tobacco and alcohol cessation. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 145"},{"q":"A 5-year-old has a few hours of high fever, drooling, refusal to swallow and a muffled voice. He sits leaning forward with his chin thrust out, looking anxious.","choices":["Do not examine the throat; secure the airway with the team","Examine the pharynx with a tongue depressor","Obtain a lateral neck radiograph first","Give oral antibiotics and review tomorrow"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The four Ds and the tripod position describe supraglottitis, and once suspected, examinations that raise the child's anxiety, including intraoral examination and venipuncture, can precipitate complete obstruction. The priority is a controlled airway in theatre with anaesthetic and surgical teams present. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 63"},{"q":"A 3-month-old breastfed infant has white plaques on the tongue and buccal mucosa that wipe off. He is thriving.","choices":["Oral fluconazole","Urgent immunodeficiency workup","Topical corticosteroid","Nystatin suspension"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Thrush is common in infants whose oral flora and immunity are still establishing, and topical antifungal therapy is the appropriate first step. Nystatin acts locally with negligible systemic absorption, which is the right profile for a well infant with superficial infection. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 90"},{"q":"A 61-year-old woman has a tumour of a minor salivary gland of the palate. She asks how likely it is to be benign.","choices":["Only about 35 per cent are benign","About 80 per cent are benign","Essentially all are benign","The site makes no difference"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The smaller the gland, the higher the malignant proportion: parotid tumours are 75 to 80 per cent benign, submandibular 50 to 60 per cent, and minor salivary glands only about 35 per cent. That inverse relationship changes how aggressively a small-gland lesion is investigated. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 139"},{"q":"A 63-year-old man with a suspicious oral lesion is being worked up.","choices":["Imaging alone without tissue","Serology alone","Biopsy, with imaging and dental evaluation","Biopsy alone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The workup includes labs with high-risk human papillomavirus testing and in situ hybridisation, computed tomography or magnetic resonance for the primary and nodes, chest X-ray and positron emission tomography for metastases, flexible fiberoptic endoscopy, biopsy and dental evaluation before treatment. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 147"},{"q":"A 46-year-old man has recurrent painful swelling under the right jaw that starts within minutes of eating and settles over the next hour. A firm structure is palpable in the floor of the mouth.","choices":["A parotid neoplasm","Submandibular sialolithiasis","Acute suppurative sialadenitis","Mumps parotitis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Eating stimulates salivary flow, and an obstructing stone traps that saliva, distending the gland until pressure equalises. Between 80 and 90 per cent of stones are submandibular because Wharton duct runs a long uphill course carrying alkaline mucin-rich saliva. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 42"},{"q":"A 31-year-old man has a painful lower wisdom tooth that has only partly erupted, with inflamed gum overlying it.","choices":["Malocclusion","Pulpitis","Impaction, which predisposes to infection","Dental caries of the wisdom tooth"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Impaction usually results from overcrowding with insufficient room to erupt, and wisdom teeth are the usual culprits because they come through last. A partially erupted tooth leaves a gum flap that traps debris, which is why impacted teeth become infected and are usually removed. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 125"},{"q":"A 60-year-old man has a slowly enlarging painless mass at the tail of the parotid, with no facial weakness.","choices":["A benign parotid tumour","A malignant parotid tumour","A minor salivary gland tumour","A parotid abscess"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Between 64 and 80 per cent of salivary neoplasms arise in the parotid and 75 to 80 per cent of those are benign, most commonly epithelial. Benign parotid tumours characteristically present as slow-growing painless masses at the tail of the gland, and the absence of facial nerve involvement supports it. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 141"},{"q":"A 28-year-old woman has recurrent painful lesions at the vermilion border of the lip, always preceded by a day of burning and tingling, triggered by sunlight and stress.","choices":["An autoimmune photosensitive reaction","Bacterial superinfection of chapped lips","Reactivation of virus latent in the trigeminal ganglion","A new primary infection each time"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. After primary infection the virus travels along the axon to the trigeminal ganglion and persists. Reactivation follows stress, trauma, immunosuppression or ultraviolet light, and the virus migrates back down the axonal sheath, which is why burning and tingling precede any visible lesion by about 24 hours. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 4-year-old with recurrent respiratory papillomatosis is deteriorating, and tracheostomy is suggested.","choices":["Give intralesional cidofovir first line","Avoid tracheostomy","Proceed with tracheostomy","Give systemic antivirals to cure the virus"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Papillomas favour squamociliary junctions, the boundaries where ciliated respiratory epithelium meets squamous epithelium. A tracheostomy creates a new one, giving the disease a fresh site to seed and spreading it distally into the trachea, which is why it is specifically avoided. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 9-year-old develops migratory joint pains and a new cardiac murmur three weeks after an untreated sore throat.","choices":["Rheumatic fever from cross-reactive antibodies","Infective endocarditis from bacterial seeding","Juvenile idiopathic arthritis","Scarlet fever"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Antibodies produced against the streptococcus cross-react with heart muscle, causing endocarditis, myocarditis or pericarditis. Signs appear 2 to 3 weeks after the infection, occasionally as early as one or as late as five, with peak incidence between 5 and 15 years. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 78"},{"q":"A 44-year-old man has a stone palpable in the anterior portion of the submandibular duct.","choices":["Excision of the submandibular gland","Lithotripsy under interventional radiology","Conservative management only","Intraoral extraction"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Management depends on the location and size of the stone. One that can be palpated or visualised in the anterior portion of the submandibular duct can be extracted intraorally, whereas larger stones in the hilum or body of the gland require excision of the gland itself. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 43"},{"q":"A 52-year-old smoker has constant throat clearing, a dry throat and odynophagia. Examination shows a thickened, granular posterior pharyngeal wall with crusting.","choices":["Laryngopharyngeal reflux alone, needing no examination","Infectious mononucleosis","Chronic pharyngitis, and address the underlying cause","Acute bacterial pharyngitis needing penicillin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Causes include postnasal drip from chronic rhinosinusitis, irritants such as dust, dry heat, chemicals, smoking and alcohol, chronic mouth breathing, voice abuse, allergy, granulomatous disease, connective tissue disorder and malignancy. Management addresses the underlying disorder and avoids precipitants, with culture and biopsy if therapy fails. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 80"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man has chronic unilateral parotid swelling with minimal pain for several months. He has tuberculosis risk factors.","choices":["Chronic granulomatous sialadenitis","Acute suppurative sialadenitis","Sialolithiasis","Mumps parotitis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Chronic unilateral or bilateral swelling with minimal pain is the picture, and primary tuberculosis should be considered where risk factors exist. The differential is wide, including cat scratch disease, sarcoidosis, actinomycosis, granulomatosis with polyangiitis and syphilis, so fine needle aspiration biopsy is needed. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 34"},{"q":"A student asks why a retropharyngeal abscess is so dangerous compared with a peritonsillar one.","choices":["It always becomes Ludwig angina","Its space extends to the posterior mediastinum","It always involves the carotid artery","It causes trismus that prevents intubation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The retropharyngeal space runs from the base of the skull to the posterior mediastinum, giving infection a direct anatomical route into the chest. Mediastinitis carries 50 per cent mortality, and the danger space beyond is continuous left to right. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 107"},{"q":"A 21-year-old rugby player is confirmed to have mononucleosis and asks when he can train again.","choices":["No restriction is needed","Avoid all exercise for six months","Avoid contact sport for about a month","Return once the fever settles"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Epstein-Barr virus enlarges the spleen, and a capsule under tension can rupture with minor abdominal trauma. The restriction runs until splenomegaly has resolved, monitored by ultrasound, because feeling well and having a normal spleen are not the same thing. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 86"},{"q":"A 56-year-old woman has lacy white lines on both buccal mucosae that cannot be wiped away. She asks whether it is dangerous.","choices":["It is an infection that antifungals will clear","It carries a 1 to 4 per cent risk of carcinoma, higher if ulcerative","It is entirely benign and needs no follow-up","It becomes malignant in most cases"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Oral lichen planus is a chronic autoimmune condition in which activated lymphocytes destroy the basal layer, and 1 to 4 per cent progress to squamous cell carcinoma, with higher risk in ulcerative forms. Close follow-up is the point of the diagnosis, alongside pain relief and removing reversible contributors. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 36-year-old woman has facial pain, jaw clicking and difficulty opening her mouth wide. She also reports tinnitus, ear fullness and dizziness. Otoscopy and audiometry are normal.","choices":["Eustachian tube dysfunction","Acoustic neuroma","Temporomandibular joint disorder","Meniere disease"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The joint sits immediately anterior to the ear canal, so its disorders commonly refer symptoms to the ear with a completely normal ear examination. Jaw pain with clicking and limited opening in a woman of childbearing age is typical, and it is the second commonest musculoskeletal cause of pain and disability. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 130"}]};
